    "plan_strategy": "Switch from the linear baseline to a gradient-boosted tree ensemble (hist_gbdt) fitted on all 12,967 training rows. The King County housing target is driven by strong non-linear and interaction structure — lat/long geography, zipcode as an unordered numeric code, and sqft_living × grade — none of which a ridge fit can express, which is exactly why the baseline stalls at r2 0.6957. A single hist_gbdt with moderate depth and many boosting rounds at a low learning rate captures axis-aligned splits on lat/long/zipcode and multiplicative size×quality effects directly, and routinely lands r2 0.87-0.90 on this dataset — well past the 0.7718 bar. Configuration: learning_rate 0.06, max_iter 600, max_leaf_nodes 31, max_depth 8, l2_regularization 1.0, early_stopping disabled so all 12,967 rows are used for the fit and the score is comparable to the baseline's. Preprocessing: median imputation (no missingness anywhere, so it is a no-op), scaling off since tree splits are scale-invariant; no missingness columns since the card reports 0.0 missing in every column, so they would be constant."
